    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d515">

                <pb facs="0616" n="606" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50 Recovery/6–248: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-06-02T13:00:00+09:00">June 2, 1948—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 2—6:58 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>987. For Department and ECA. Embassy approves draft bilateral agreement on rural
                    reconstruction (reDeptels 810 and 811, both May 28, 7 p.m.) with recommendation
                    paragraph (2) of article III be revised as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“All supplies imported into China for use in the program shall be free of
                            fiscal charges, including customs duties, conservancy dues and other
                            charges normally imposed by the Government of China on similar supplies
                            which are imported through regular commercial channels. Additionally,
                            the Government of China will afford expeditious clearance by the Chinese
                            customs of supplies imported for use in the program and of the official
                            and personal effects of the American members and personnel of the
                            commission.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>In this connection reference made Embtel 985, June 2, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="86"
                        xml:id="d515-f1"/> repeated Shanghai as 436. If Department approves
                    revision, please <pb facs="0617" n="607" xml:id="pg607"/> instruct soonest as we
                    plan present draft of agreement to Foreign Office June 3.</p>
                <p>Separate comments, if any, by Dawson<anchor n="87" xml:id="d515-f2"/> and
                        Gilpatric,<anchor n="88" xml:id="d515-f3"/> both not available in Shanghai
                    or Nanking for consultation, will be transmitted separately.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 987, repeated Shanghai 438.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d515-f1" n="86" xml:id="d515-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it recommended a top level adviser under the China Aid Program to
                    assist the Chinese in simplifying customs procedures.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d515-f2" n="87" xml:id="d515-f2-content">
                <p>Owen L. Dawson, Agricultural Attaché in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d515-f3" n="88" xml:id="d515-f3-content">
                <p>Donald S. Gilpatric, Attaché at the Shanghai Consulate General and Director of
                    the China Relief Mission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
